WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.800
So numbers in Python, these are all integers, and we can always verify that by calling

00:14.800 --> 00:31.280
type, there are also floats in Python, so numbers that have decimal points.

00:31.280 --> 00:37.640
Something that's a little tricky that might bite you, this is a valid float.

00:37.640 --> 00:40.920
You don't need to have a decimal point after.

00:40.920 --> 00:45.600
You shouldn't really define your floats like this, it's confusing, but if you see this

00:45.600 --> 00:49.560
in Python code, just know that that's not a syntax error.

00:49.560 --> 00:57.000
And then there are also complex numbers, and these end with the character j.

00:57.000 --> 01:01.280
I'm not a math person, I don't think I've ever used complex numbers in my Python code,

01:01.280 --> 01:05.960
but they're there if you need them.

01:06.200 --> 01:11.800
Like you saw earlier, these types are just objects under the hood, you can also declare

01:11.800 --> 01:21.480
them by calling the constructor for that built-in type, so I can say into five.

01:21.480 --> 01:27.960
I can also pass in a string in here, same thing for float.

01:27.960 --> 01:34.200
If I pass in, let's say an integer into the float constructor, we'll return a float back.

01:34.200 --> 01:39.160
Generally, you never want to do this in your code unless you are converting between

01:39.160 --> 01:44.640
types, but this is something that you might see.

01:44.640 --> 01:49.800
One example of converting between types is if you're using an old-style string formatting,

01:49.800 --> 01:58.440
if you try to, for example, concatenate, we'll talk about strings a little bit later, a string

01:58.440 --> 02:03.360
with a number, you're going to get a type error that says you can only concatenate strings

02:03.440 --> 02:10.080
and not ins, so in this case, you can pass in the number five to the stir method.

02:10.080 --> 02:17.680
Again, with modern Python string formatting, you won't really ever need to do this, but

02:17.680 --> 02:21.440
could happen.

02:21.440 --> 02:26.120
You can do all the standard mathematical operations that you might expect with Python

02:26.120 --> 02:33.160
numbers, you can add them up and you can multiply them.

02:33.160 --> 02:38.200
You can divide them, note that if you divide two integers, the type that you get back

02:38.200 --> 02:39.960
is going to be a float.

02:39.960 --> 02:45.600
If you want to do integer division, you need to use two slashes, and that will get you

02:45.600 --> 02:47.760
an integer back.

02:47.760 --> 02:55.880
You can also raise to the powers, so let's say we have X equals four and Y equals three,

02:55.880 --> 03:01.800
you can say X to the power of three or X to the power of Y.

03:01.800 --> 03:08.320
So everything that you might expect, and then you can also use parentheses to make sure

03:08.320 --> 03:18.600
that things happen in the order that you want, and that is all a valid Python code.

03:18.600 --> 03:25.640
Python has a few more built-in methods that are useful for numbers, and you might hear

03:25.640 --> 03:28.800
me say built-in methods, what does that mean?

03:28.840 --> 03:34.320
You saw earlier in our tour of a Python program that I had to import the request module.

03:34.320 --> 03:38.600
Later on, we'll see how to import modules from the standard library.

03:38.600 --> 03:41.960
When I'm using these built-ins, I don't need to import anything.

03:41.960 --> 03:45.480
They're just available in Python.

03:45.480 --> 03:52.000
And the three helpful methods for math are min and max or rounds.

03:52.000 --> 04:01.000
So for min, you could pass in some values, and it will always return the smallest of them.

04:01.000 --> 04:06.160
The same is true for max, except the opposite.

04:06.160 --> 04:11.120
This will return 10,000, because it is the largest number in that list.

04:11.120 --> 04:20.080
You can also use round to round down decimals, or round them up.

04:20.080 --> 04:26.440
There are built-in blend types in Python, just like we saw that none is a keyword.

04:26.440 --> 04:33.760
In Python, the blend types are defined with uppercase true, and lowercase false.

04:33.760 --> 04:36.200
These are keywords.

04:36.200 --> 04:42.320
Something of note in Python that I've seen tripped people up, again, in Python, everything

04:42.320 --> 04:43.520
is an object.

04:43.520 --> 04:54.360
These booleans are objects, and, in fact, under the hood, they are subclasses of integers.

04:54.360 --> 05:00.600
That means that under the hood, true has a value of 1, and false has a value of 0.

05:00.600 --> 05:05.680
Don't do this, please.

05:05.680 --> 05:13.480
Because of that, you can actually add these together or do anything that you could do to numbers.

